---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About Dr. DuBose — DuBose, M.D." active="about">

  <!-- 1 · Introduction -->
  <section class="section" style="padding-block:clamp(56px,8vw,96px)">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="about-grid">
        <div class="photo-frame">
          <div class="photo" style="aspect-ratio:4/5">
            <img src="/images/dr-dubose.jpg" alt="Dr. Logan DuBose" width="800" height="1000" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <span class="eyebrow">About me</span>
          <h1 style="font-size:clamp(30px,4vw,44px)">Logan DuBose, M.D.</h1>
          <p style="margin-top:20px">
            I&rsquo;m a physician and researcher. I&rsquo;ve spent my career
            in two places at once: practicing primary care, and leading
            research on why healthcare so often fails the people who need it
            most. This practice is where those two careers meet — one doctor,
            a small panel, and enough time to do medicine properly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 · Why I practice this way -->
  <section class="section band-warm">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">The path</span>
          <h2>Why I practice this way</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            My path isn&rsquo;t the usual one, so let me tell it to you
            straight. I earned my M.D. at the Texas A&amp;M College of
            Medicine and completed an internal medicine internship at George
            Washington University. Then, instead of continuing down the
            standard hospital track, I made a bet on a question that had
            bothered me through all of training: why does a system with this
            much medicine in it leave so many people feeling unseen?
          </p>
          <p>
            I spent the next years working on that question — leading
            research funded by the National Institutes of Health on aging,
            caregiving, and access to care, and building tools to help
            families navigate a system that seems designed to be
            unnavigable. The whole time, I kept practicing primary care,
            including as a volunteer physician at a charity health center,
            where I cared for people the system had priced out entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here is what years of studying healthcare&rsquo;s failures taught
            me: most people are not failed by a lack of medicine. They are
            failed by fragmentation — fifteen-minute visits, results that
            arrive without explanation, five doctors and no one holding the
            whole picture. I have measured that problem, published on it, and
            watched it happen to people I cared about.
          </p>
          <p>
            This practice is my answer. Not a company, not a platform — a
            return to the simplest unit of medicine: one physician and one
            person, with enough time between them. I keep the panel small and
            the overhead near zero so the price can stay at $100 a month and
            the visits can be as long as they need to be. I practice
            alongside my continuing research on healthy aging — each one
            makes me better at the other.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 · About experience -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">Straight answers</span>
          <h2>About experience — stated plainly</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I believe trust starts with precision about qualifications, so
            here is mine without varnish: I completed medical school and an
            internal medicine internship, and I have practiced primary care
            part-time for years while leading research full-time. I have not
            spent decades in an exam room, and I won&rsquo;t pretend
            otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            What I promise instead is a way of practicing: I work from
            evidence. I look things up rather than guess. I think before I
            prescribe. I say &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; when I
            don&rsquo;t — and then I find out. And I refer early and without
            ego, because knowing when a problem needs a specialist, an urgent
            care visit, or an emergency room is one of the most important
            skills in primary care. This practice is built around those
            habits: time to think, time to investigate, and no financial
            pressure to do anything but what your situation needs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 · Training and credentials -->
  <section class="section band-warm">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">The record</span>
        <h2>Training and credentials</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rows">
        <div class="row"><b>M.D.</b><p>Texas A&amp;M College of Medicine</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Internship</b><p>Internal medicine, George Washington University</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Licensure</b><p>Virginia (unrestricted), DEA-registered; Texas licensure [at launch]</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Clinical practice</b><p>Part-time primary care; volunteer general practitioner, Fredericksburg Christian Health Center</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Research</b><p>Chief Research Officer, Olera; NIH-funded research on aging, caregiving, and access to care; Affiliate Faculty, Texas A&amp;M School of Public Health, Center for Community Health &amp; Aging</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>In progress</b><p>Lifestyle medicine certification, International Board of Lifestyle Medicine</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Education</b><p>B.S. Biochemistry, The University of Texas at Austin; M.B.A., Texas A&amp;M University</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 · How I practice -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Commitments</span>
        <h2>How I practice</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rows">
        <div class="row">
          <b>Evidence first</b>
          <p>I recommend what good evidence supports. When the evidence is
          thin or mixed, I say so, and we decide together with that honesty
          on the table.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>I explain my reasoning</b>
          <p>You will know not just what I recommend but why — and what
          I&rsquo;d expect to happen if we chose differently.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>I say &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; when I don&rsquo;t</b>
          <p>Then I find out, or I find the person who knows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>I refer without ego</b>
          <p>When someone else is the right doctor for part of your care,
          you&rsquo;ll be in their office — with me still watching the whole
          picture.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Your decisions are yours</b>
          <p>My job is to make sure they are informed, unrushed, and
          genuinely yours.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 · Outside the practice -->
  <section class="section band-warm">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">Off the clock</span>
          <h2>Outside the practice</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I&rsquo;m married to a pediatrician I met in medical school — our
            house settles most medical debates by specialty. I&rsquo;ve
            played jazz since high school and still do; I read and write; and
            my wife and I will take any excuse to travel, find live comedy,
            or get to a beach. Some of the most meaningful medicine
            I&rsquo;ve done has been on medical mission trips, which taught
            me how much good a doctor can do with very little besides time
            and attention. That lesson is half of why this practice exists.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 · CTA -->
  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>The best way to know if we&rsquo;d work well together is to talk.</h2>
        <p>
          Thirty minutes, by video, free — and if I&rsquo;m not the right
          fit, I&rsquo;ll say so and point you toward someone good.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn-inverse" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
